---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} ogType="article" alternates={["en"]}>
    <SeoHead title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} type="WebPage" />
    <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleJsonLd)} />
    <Nav />
    <Breadcrumb
        items={[
            { label: "Home", href: "/" },
            { label: "Guides", href: "/guides" },
            { label: "Loguru for Python" },
        ]}
    />

    <main>
        <header class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-border pt-[19px]">
            <div
                class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0"
                aria-hidden="true"
                style="background-image: linear-gradient(to right, oklch(1 0 0 / 4%) 1px, transparent 1px), linear-gradient(to bottom, oklch(1 0 0 / 4%) 1px, transparent 1px); background-size: 60px 60px; mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 78% 68% at 22% 26%, black, transparent);"
            >
            </div>
            <div class="relative mx-auto grid max-w-6xl gap-12 px-6 pb-16 pt-20 md:pb-24 md:pt-28 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1.05fr)_minmax(340px,0.75fr)] lg:items-center lg:gap-16">
                <div>
                    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-fg-muted">
                        <span class="border border-primary/40 px-2 py-1 text-primary">Logging</span>
                        <span>15 min read</span>
                    </div>
                    <h1 class="mt-6 max-w-[16ch] font-display text-[clamp(40px,6.3vw,72px)] font-medium leading-[0.98] tracking-[-0.045em] text-fg">
                        Loguru for Python: setup, sinks, JSON and OpenTelemetry
                    </h1>
                    <p class="mt-7 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-fg-muted md:text-base">
                        Install Loguru, write rotating files and JSON, attach request context, route the standard <code>logging</code> module through it, and forward every record to OpenTelemetry with its trace ID.
                    </p>
                    <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap gap-x-5 gap-y-2 font-mono text-[10px] text-fg-muted">
                        <span>By Maple</span>
                        <time datetime={published}>Updated {updatedLabel}</time>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-bg-deep p-4 shadow-2xl shadow-black/20 md:p-5" aria-label="Loguru at a glance">
                    <div class="flex items-center justify-between border-b border-border pb-3 font-mono text-[10px] text-fg-muted">
                        <span>loguru / at a glance</span>
                        <span class="text-primary">0.7.3</span>
                    </div>
                    <ol class="divide-y divide-border">
                        {[
                            ["01", "Import", "from loguru import logger", "no setup"],
                            ["02", "Files", "rotation, retention, compression", "logger.add()"],
                            ["03", "JSON", "serialize=True", "one line per record"],
                            ["04", "Context", "bind() and contextualize()", "lands in extra"],
                            ["05", "OpenTelemetry", "a sink into a stdlib LoggingHandler", "your code"],
                        ].map(([number, label, headline, detail]) => (
                            <li class="grid grid-cols-[2rem_1fr] gap-3 py-3.5">
                                <span class="font-mono text-[10px] text-primary">{number}</span>
                                <div>
                                    <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
                                        <span class="text-xs font-medium text-fg">{label}</span>
                                        <span class="font-mono text-[9px] text-fg-muted">{detail}</span>
                                    </div>
                                    <p class="mt-1 font-mono text-[11px] leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">{headline}</p>
                                </div>
                            </li>
                        ))}
                    </ol>
                </div>
            </div>
        </header>

        <nav aria-label="Guide sections" class="border-b border-border bg-bg-deep">
            <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-6xl gap-6 overflow-x-auto px-6 py-4 font-mono text-[10px] whitespace-nowrap text-fg-muted">
                {toc.map(([id, label]) => (
                    <a href={`#${id}`} class="transition-colors hover:text-primary">{label}</a>
                ))}
            </div>
        </nav>

        <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl gap-12 px-6 py-16 md:py-24 lg:grid-cols-[14rem_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:gap-16">
            <aside class="hidden lg:block">
                <TableOfContents headings={tocHeadings} class="sticky top-28" />
            </aside>

            <article class="loguru-guide min-w-0">
                <section id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow">01 · Install</p>
                    <h2>Install Loguru with pip</h2>

                    <div class="guide-answer">
                        <p>
                            <strong>Loguru</strong> is an MIT-licensed Python logging library built around one pre-configured <code>logger</code> object. Every destination, filter and format is set with a single method, <code>logger.add()</code>. The current release is 0.7.3 and it supports Python 3.5 and later.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <Code code={installCode} lang="bash" theme={codeTheme} />

                    <p>
                        <code>ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'loguru'</code> almost always means the package went into a different interpreter than the one running your code. Running pip through <code>python -m pip</code> installs into the interpreter you call, so use the same <code>python</code> that starts the app, and make sure your editor and process manager point at the same virtual environment.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="default-logger" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow">02 · First log line</p>
                    <h2>The Loguru logger works on import</h2>
                    <p>
                        <code>from loguru import logger</code> gives you a logger with one handler already attached: colored output to <code>sys.stderr</code>, level <code>DEBUG</code> and above. There is no <code>getLogger(__name__)</code> step; Loguru reads the caller's module name from the stack.
                    </p>

                    <Code code={quickstartCode} lang="python" theme={codeTheme} />
                    <Code code={quickstartOutput} lang="text" theme={codeTheme} />

                    <p>
                        Messages use <code>str.format</code> placeholders, so <code>{"{}"}</code> and <code>{"{name}"}</code> work and <code>%s</code> does not. Keyword arguments fill named placeholders and are also stored on the record's <code>extra</code> dict, which matters once you add JSON output.
                    </p>

                    <h3>Set the log level</h3>
                    <p>
                        The default handler cannot change level after it is added. Remove it and add your own:
                    </p>
                    <Code code={setLevelCode} lang="python" theme={codeTheme} />
                    <p>
                        <code>logger.remove()</code> with no argument removes every handler. <code>logger.add()</code> returns an integer ID, and <code>logger.remove(handler_id)</code> removes just that one. To change the default handler's level without touching code, start the process with <code>LOGURU_LEVEL=INFO</code>. In place of <code>level</code>, a dict filter sets a minimum per module, here <code>INFO</code> overall and <code>WARNING</code> for a chatty dependency:
                    </p>
                    <Code code={filterCode} lang="python" theme={codeTheme} />
                </section>

                <section id="file-sinks" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow">03 · Sinks</p>
                    <h2>Log to a file with rotation, retention and compression</h2>
                    <p>
                        A <strong>sink</strong> is wherever a handler writes: a file path, a stream such as <code>sys.stderr</code>, a function, a coroutine, or a standard <code>logging.Handler</code>. Passing a path creates a file sink, and file sinks take the options that a <code>RotatingFileHandler</code> setup would otherwise need.
                    </p>

                    <Code code={fileCode} lang="python" theme={codeTheme} />

                    <div class="guide-table-wrap">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Option</th><th>Accepts</th><th>Example</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>rotation</code></td><td>A size, a time of day, an interval, a weekday, or a function</td><td><code>"100 MB"</code>, <code>"00:00"</code>, <code>"1 week"</code>, <code>"monday at 12:00"</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>retention</code></td><td>A maximum age, or a number of files to keep</td><td><code>"14 days"</code>, <code>"1 month"</code>, <code>10</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>compression</code></td><td>An archive format applied to each rotated file</td><td><code>"gz"</code>, <code>"bz2"</code>, <code>"xz"</code>, <code>"zip"</code>, <code>"tar.gz"</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>delay</code></td><td>Create the file on the first message instead of at <code>add()</code></td><td><code>True</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>level</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>format</code></td><td>The same options every sink takes</td><td><code>level="ERROR"</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <p>
                        On rotation Loguru renames the current file with a timestamp, for example <code>app.2026-10-19_14-03-12_511234.log.gz</code>, and opens a fresh <code>app.log</code>. Retention only counts files that match the sink's naming pattern, so the two sinks above clean up independently. The path itself can contain a time placeholder such as <code>"logs/app_{"{time:YYYY-MM-DD}"}.log"</code>.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="levels" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow">04 · Levels</p>
                    <h2>Loguru levels and custom levels</h2>
                    <p>
                        Loguru keeps the standard library's numbers and adds two levels, <code>TRACE</code> below <code>DEBUG</code> and <code>SUCCESS</code> between <code>INFO</code> and <code>WARNING</code>.
                    </p>

                    <div class="guide-table-wrap">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Level</th><th>Number</th><th>Method</th><th>Standard <code>logging</code></th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>TRACE</code></td><td>5</td><td><code>logger.trace()</code></td><td>None</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>DEBUG</code></td><td>10</td><td><code>logger.debug()</code></td><td><code>DEBUG</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>INFO</code></td><td>20</td><td><code>logger.info()</code></td><td><code>INFO</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>SUCCESS</code></td><td>25</td><td><code>logger.success()</code></td><td>None</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>WARNING</code></td><td>30</td><td><code>logger.warning()</code></td><td><code>WARNING</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>ERROR</code></td><td>40</td><td><code>logger.error()</code>, <code>logger.exception()</code></td><td><code>ERROR</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>CRITICAL</code></td><td>50</td><td><code>logger.critical()</code></td><td><code>CRITICAL</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <p>
                        A handler's <code>level</code> is a minimum: <code>level="INFO"</code> passes <code>INFO</code>, <code>SUCCESS</code> and everything above. Register a custom level with a number, then log to it by name:
                    </p>
                    <Code code={customLevelCode} lang="python" theme={codeTheme} />
                    <p>
                        Numbers matter when records leave Loguru. OpenTelemetry maps level numbers to severities, so <code>SUCCESS</code> (25) exports as <code>INFO4</code> and a custom level at 35 as <code>WARN4</code>. The <a href="/guides/python-logging-levels">Python logging levels guide</a> covers how to choose a level for a message.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="formatting" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow">05 · Formatting</p>
                    <h2>Format Loguru output</h2>
                    <p>
                        The <code>format</code> argument is a <code>str.format</code> template over the record. Markup tags such as <code>&lt;green&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;level&gt;</code> add color, and Loguru strips them automatically when the sink is a file or a stream that is not a terminal.
                    </p>
                    <Code code={formatCode} lang="python" theme={codeTheme} />
                    <Code code={formatOutput} lang="text" theme={codeTheme} />

                    <p>
                        Useful record fields: <code>time</code> (with a format spec such as <code>{"{time:YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm:ss.SSS}"}</code>), <code>level</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>module</code>, <code>function</code>, <code>line</code>, <code>file</code>, <code>process</code>, <code>thread</code>, <code>elapsed</code>, <code>message</code>, <code>extra</code> and <code>exception</code>. A single key from <code>extra</code> is <code>{"{extra[request_id]}"}</code>.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <code>logger.opt()</code> changes one call. <code>opt(lazy=True)</code> takes functions instead of values and only calls them if some handler accepts the level, so an expensive debug summary costs nothing at <code>INFO</code>:
                    </p>
                    <Code code={lazyCode} lang="python" theme={codeTheme} />
                </section>

                <section id="json" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow">06 · Structured output</p>
                    <h2>JSON logs with serialize=True</h2>
                    <p>
                        <code>serialize=True</code> turns any sink into JSON lines. Each line has the formatted <code>text</code> and the full <code>record</code> (trimmed below):
                    </p>
                    <Code code={serializeCode} lang="python" theme={codeTheme} />
                    <Code code={serializeOutput} lang="json" theme={codeTheme} />

                    <p>
                        That document is complete but verbose, and log pipelines usually want a flat object with the context at the top level. A function sink gives you that. Loguru calls it with the formatted message, and <code>message.record</code> holds the same dict the format string reads from:
                    </p>
                    <Code code={jsonSinkCode} lang="python" theme={codeTheme} />
                    <Code code={jsonSinkOutput} lang="json" theme={codeTheme} />
                </section>

                <section id="context" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow">07 · Context</p>
                    <h2>Structured context with bind and contextualize</h2>
                    <p>
                        <code>logger.bind()</code> returns a new logger that adds fixed values to <code>extra</code> on every record. The global <code>logger</code> is unchanged, so pass the bound logger to the code that should carry the values:
                    </p>
                    <Code code={bindCode} lang="python" theme={codeTheme} />

                    <p>
                        <code>logger.contextualize()</code> sets values for everything logged inside a <code>with</code> block, including calls in other functions that use the plain global <code>logger</code>. It is stored in a <code>contextvars</code> variable, so concurrent asyncio tasks and threads each see their own values. That makes it the right tool for a request ID set once in middleware.
                    </p>
                    <Code code={contextualizeCode} lang="python" theme={codeTheme} />
                    <Code code={contextualizeOutput} lang="text" theme={codeTheme} />

                    <p>
                        <code>logger.configure(extra=...)</code> sets a default. Without it, a format that references <code>{"{extra[request_id]}"}</code> raises a <code>KeyError</code> for any record logged outside the block, and Loguru prints a logging error to stderr instead of the line.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="exceptions" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow">08 · Exceptions</p>
                    <h2>logger.catch and exception formatting</h2>
                    <p>
                        <code>@logger.catch</code> wraps a function (or, as <code>with logger.catch():</code>, a block), logs any exception with its traceback at <code>ERROR</code>, and swallows it. It suits the entry point of a script or a worker thread, where an uncaught exception would otherwise disappear. <code>@logger.catch(reraise=True)</code> logs and then re-raises. Inside an <code>except</code> block, <code>logger.exception()</code> logs the current exception.
                    </p>
                    <Code code={catchCode} lang="python" theme={codeTheme} />

                    <p>
                        Two sink options control the traceback. <code>backtrace=True</code> extends it past the point where the exception was caught. <code>diagnose=True</code> annotates each frame with the values of its variables:
                    </p>
                    <Code code={diagnoseCode} lang="python" theme={codeTheme} />
                    <Code code={diagnoseOutput} lang="text" theme={codeTheme} />

                    <div class="guide-callout">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">Turn diagnose off in production</p>
                        <p>
                            Both options default to <code>True</code>. The variable values that make <code>diagnose</code> useful locally are request bodies, tokens and customer data in production, and the Loguru README warns that it can leak sensitive data there. Add the production sink with <code>diagnose=False</code>, or set <code>LOGURU_DIAGNOSE=NO</code> for the default handler.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <Code code={productionSinkCode} lang="python" theme={codeTheme} />
                </section>

                <section id="concurrency" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow">09 · Concurrency</p>
                    <h2>Threads, asyncio and multiprocessing with enqueue=True</h2>
                    <p>
                        Loguru sinks are thread-safe by default. <code>enqueue=True</code> adds a queue between the logging call and the sink. A background thread drains the queue, so the call returns without waiting for disk or network I/O, and because the queue is a multiprocessing queue, several processes can write to one file without interleaving lines.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Child processes need the configured logger. With the <code>spawn</code> start method (the default on macOS and Windows) and <code>forkserver</code> (the Linux default since Python 3.14), a child imports your module again and gets a fresh default logger, so pass the parent's logger to each worker:
                    </p>
                    <Code code={enqueueCode} lang="python" theme={codeTheme} />
                    <p>
                        <code>logger.complete()</code> waits until the queue is empty. Call it before the process exits, or the last records can be lost.
                    </p>

                    <h3>Async sinks</h3>
                    <p>
                        A coroutine function can be a sink. Loguru schedules it on the running event loop, and <code>await logger.complete()</code> waits for the pending calls to finish:
                    </p>
                    <Code code={asyncCode} lang="python" theme={codeTheme} />
                </section>

                <section id="intercept" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow">10 · Standard library</p>
                    <h2>Route the standard logging module into Loguru</h2>
                    <p>
                        Your dependencies (requests, SQLAlchemy, botocore, uvicorn) log through the standard <code>logging</code> module and never call Loguru. An <code>InterceptHandler</code> on the root logger forwards their records into Loguru, so one set of sinks handles everything:
                    </p>
                    <Code code={interceptCode} lang="python" theme={codeTheme} />

                    <p>
                        <code>level=0</code> lets every record reach Loguru, where the handler levels decide what is written. <code>force=True</code> removes handlers that something else already put on the root logger. The <code>depth</code> walk makes the record report the line in your code or the library, instead of a line inside <code>logging/__init__.py</code>.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Loggers that set <code>propagate = False</code> with their own handlers never reach the root. uvicorn configures its loggers that way, so clear them after it starts, for example at import time in your app module:
                    </p>
                    <Code code={uvicornCode} lang="python" theme={codeTheme} />
                </section>

                <section id="opentelemetry" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow">11 · OpenTelemetry</p>
                    <h2>Send Loguru logs to OpenTelemetry</h2>
                    <p>
                        The OpenTelemetry Python SDK collects logs through a <code>LoggingHandler</code> attached to the standard <code>logging</code> module. Records logged inside an active span get its trace and span IDs, which is what links a log line to its trace. The <a href="/docs/guides/instrumentation-python">Python instrumentation guide</a> sets this up in a <code>telemetry.py</code> module. Loguru writes to its own sinks and never touches that handler, so Loguru output does not reach OpenTelemetry on its own.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The bridge below is a Loguru sink that rebuilds each Loguru record as a <code>logging.LogRecord</code> and hands it to a dedicated stdlib logger carrying the OpenTelemetry handler. It is a short piece of your own application code, not a Maple feature or an OpenTelemetry package, and it works with any OTLP backend.
                    </p>

                    <Code code={otelInstallCode} lang="bash" theme={codeTheme} />
                    <p>
                        Start from <code>telemetry.py</code> in the Python guide and delete its last line, the one that adds a <code>LoggingHandler</code> to the root logger. With the <code>InterceptHandler</code> from the previous section installed, standard-library records already travel through Loguru, and this sink sends each record to OpenTelemetry exactly once:
                    </p>
                    <Code code={bridgeCode} lang="python" theme={codeTheme} />

                    <div class="guide-grid guide-grid--two">
                        <div>
                            <p class="guide-kicker">Keep the sink synchronous</p>
                            <h3>No enqueue=True on this sink</h3>
                            <p>The handler reads the active span when it runs. Run from Loguru's queue thread, it finds no span and every record exports with an all-zero trace ID. The SDK's batch processor already exports in the background.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <p class="guide-kicker">Filter the SDK's own logs</p>
                            <h3>Keep export errors out of the pipeline</h3>
                            <p>When an export fails, the exporter logs an error, which the intercept routes back into Loguru. The <code>filter</code> keeps those records on stderr instead of queueing them for the export that just failed.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <p class="guide-kicker">Context becomes attributes</p>
                            <h3>bind() values are searchable</h3>
                            <p>Everything in <code>extra</code> becomes a log attribute. Avoid binding keys that already exist on <code>LogRecord</code>, such as <code>name</code>, <code>module</code> or <code>message</code>: <code>makeRecord</code> rejects them and Loguru prints a logging error instead.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <p class="guide-kicker">Severity text</p>
                            <h3>Custom levels keep their names</h3>
                            <p>Setting <code>levelname</code> sends <code>SUCCESS</code> or <code>AUDIT</code> as the severity text instead of <code>Level 25</code>. <code>TRACE</code> (5) sits below the standard range and exports with an unspecified severity number.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <p>
                        The handler comes from <code>opentelemetry-instrumentation-logging</code>. Recent SDK releases deprecate the copy in <code>opentelemetry.sdk._logs</code> and point to this package; both accept the same <code>logger_provider</code> argument. To check the wiring before pointing it at a backend, swap the OTLP exporter for <code>ConsoleLogRecordExporter</code> and log inside a span:
                    </p>
                    <Code code={bridgeUsageCode} lang="python" theme={codeTheme} />
                    <Code code={bridgeOutput} lang="json" theme={codeTheme} />

                    <div class="guide-maple">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">Loguru logs in Maple</p>
                        <h3>Search by trace ID, jump to the trace</h3>
                        <p>
                            With the OTLP exporter from the Python guide pointed at Maple's ingest endpoint, bridged records arrive like any other OpenTelemetry log. Open <strong>Logs</strong>, filter by service and severity in the sidebar, or paste a trace ID into the search box to see every line from one request. In a log's detail sheet, <code>order_id</code> and other bound values appear under <strong>Log Attributes</strong>, and the <code>trace:</code> link opens the request the line belongs to.
                        </p>
                        <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
                            <a href="/docs/guides/instrumentation-python" class="guide-button guide-button--primary">Set up Python with OpenTelemetry</a>
                            <a href="/docs/explore/logs" class="guide-button">Explore logs in Maple</a>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section id="comparison" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow">12 · Comparison</p>
                    <h2>Loguru vs the standard logging module</h2>

                    <div class="guide-table-wrap">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Task</th><th>Loguru</th><th>Standard <code>logging</code></th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>First log line</td><td><code>from loguru import logger</code></td><td><code>basicConfig()</code> or a handler, then <code>getLogger(__name__)</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Rotating files</td><td><code>rotation</code>, <code>retention</code>, <code>compression</code> on <code>add()</code></td><td><code>RotatingFileHandler</code> or <code>TimedRotatingFileHandler</code>; compression needs a custom <code>rotator</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>JSON output</td><td><code>serialize=True</code> or a function sink</td><td>A custom <code>Formatter</code> or a third-party package</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Context fields</td><td><code>bind()</code>, <code>contextualize()</code></td><td><code>extra=</code> per call, <code>LoggerAdapter</code>, or a filter reading a <code>ContextVar</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Exception logging</td><td><code>@logger.catch</code>, variable values with <code>diagnose</code></td><td><code>logger.exception()</code> with a standard traceback</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Multiple processes</td><td><code>enqueue=True</code></td><td><code>QueueHandler</code> and <code>QueueListener</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Message syntax</td><td><code>{"{}"}</code> placeholders</td><td><code>%s</code> placeholders</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Used by libraries</td><td>Rarely; a library should not configure global logging</td><td>Nearly all of them</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>OpenTelemetry</td><td>Needs a bridge sink</td><td><code>LoggingHandler</code> attaches directly</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Install</td><td>Third-party package</td><td>Part of Python</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <p>
                        For an application, Loguru removes most of the setup code. For a library, use the standard <code>logging</code> module so the application decides where records go, and an application on Loguru picks them up with the <code>InterceptHandler</code>.
                    </p>
                </section>

